{"id":"neet-2026r-q015","pn":15,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Gravitation","st":"Escape speed","q":"Two planets P₁ and P₂ with equal mass have radii R₁ and R₂, respectively, where R₂ = R₁/2. The escape speeds of P₁ and P₂ are v₁ and v₂, respectively. Then v₂/v₁ is:","o":["1","√2","2","1/√2"],"c":1,"s":"Escape speed v_e = √(2gR) = √(2GM/R).\nM is the same for both, so v_e ∝ 1/√R.\nv₂/v₁ = √(R₁/R₂) = √2."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q026","pn":26,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Gravitation","st":"Kepler's law of periods","q":"In a solar system, the time-period of revolution of a planet tracing a circular orbit of radius R is proportional to:","o":["R^(3/2)","R²","R³","R^(1/2)"],"c":0,"s":"In planetary motion, T² ∝ R³.\nSo T ∝ R^(3/2)."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q027","pn":27,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Gravitation","st":"Gravitational potential energy","q":"The amount of work done to raise a mass m from the surface of the Earth to a height equal to the radius of the Earth R will be","o":["2mgR","mgR","mgR/4","mgR/2"],"c":3,"s":"Work done = U₂ − U₁.\nAt height R the distance from the centre is 2R, so U₂ = −GMm/(R + R) = −GMm/(2R).\nU₁ = −GMm/R.\nW = −GMm/(2R) + GMm/R = GMm/(2R).\nUsing GM = gR², W = mgR/2."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q031","pn":31,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Gravitation","st":"Kepler's laws","q":"The radius of Martian orbit around the Sun is about 4 times the radius of the orbit of Mercury. The Martian year is 687 Earth days. Then which of the following is the length of 1 year on Mercury?","o":["88 earth days","225 earth days","172 earth days","124 earth days"],"c":0,"s":"Apply Kepler’s 3rd law: T² ∝ R³.\nRadius of Martian orbit R′ = 4R.\n(T′/T)² = (R′/R)³ = (4R/R)³ = 4³ = 64.\nSo T′/T = 8.\nLength of 1 year on Mercury T = T′/8 = 687/8 = 85.88 days.\nNote: printed answer (1) 88 days; the working gives 85.88 days, which is closest to 88 (Mercury's year is about 88 days)"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q032","pn":32,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Gravitation","st":"Variation of g with height","q":"A body weighs 48 N on the surface of the earth. The gravitational force experienced by the body due to the earth at a height equal to one-third the radius of the earth from its surface is :","o":["16 N","27 N","32 N","36 N"],"c":1,"s":"W = mg, with g = GM/R² and g_h = GM/(R+h)².\nW_h/W = mg_h/mg = g_h/g = R²/(R+h)², with h = R/3.\nW_h/W = R²/(R + R/3)² = R²/(4R/3)² = 9/16.\nW_h = (9/16)W = (9/16) × 48 (W = 48 N).\nW_h = 27 N."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q024","pn":24,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Gravitation","st":"Acceleration due to gravity","q":"The mass of a planet is (1)/(10)th that of the earth and its diameter is half that of earth. The acceleration due to gravity on that planet is-","o":["4.9 m s⁻²","3.92 m s⁻²","19.6 m s⁻²","9.8 m s⁻²"],"c":1,"s":"g′ = GM′/R′², with M′ = M/10 and R′ = R/2.\ng′ = (GM/10)/(R/2)² = (4/10)(GM/R²).\ng′ = 0.4 × 9.8 = 3.92 m s⁻²."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q049","pn":49,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Gravitation","st":"Satellite launch energy","q":"The minimum energy required to launch a satellite of mass m from the surface of earth of mass M and radius R in a circular orbit at an altitude of 2R from the surface of the earth is:","o":["GmM/(2R)","GmM/(3R)","5GmM/(6R)","2GmM/(3R)"],"c":2,"s":"Use energy conservation: U_i + K_i = U_f + K_f.\nInitially U_i = −GMm/R. At altitude 2R the orbit radius is 3R, so U_f = −GMm/(3R).\nOrbital speed at 3R satisfies v² = GM/(3R), so K_f = ½ m × GM/(3R).\n−GMm/R + K_i = −GMm/(3R) + ½ × m × GM/(3R).\nK_i = −(1/6)(GMm/R) + GMm/R.\nK_i = (5/6) GMm/R."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q010","pn":10,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Gravitation","st":"Gravitational potential and field","q":"Two bodies of mass m and 9 m are placed at a distance R. The gravitational potential on the line joining the bodies where the gravitational field equals zero, will be (G = gravitational constant):","o":["−20 Gm/R","−8 Gm/R","−12 Gm/R","−16 Gm/R"],"c":3,"s":"Let the zero-field point Q be at distance x from mass m, so R − x from mass 9m.\nFields cancel: Gm/x² = G(9m)/(R − x)².\nSo (R − x)²/x² = 9, which gives x = R/4.\nPotential at Q = −Gm/x − G(9m)/(R − x).\n= −Gm/(R/4) − 9Gm/(3R/4) = −4Gm/R − 12Gm/R = −16Gm/R.","sfig":{"w":300,"h":110,"alt":"Line of length R: mass m at the left end, 9m at the right end, point Q at distance x from m and R − x from 9m.","d":[["l",150,22,30,22,"a"],["l",150,22,270,22,"a"],["t",150,16,"R","m"],["l",30,60,270,60],["c",30,60,3,"F"],["c",90,60,3,"F"],["c",270,60,3,"F"],["t",30,50,"m","m"],["t",270,50,"9m","m"],["t",90,88,"Q","m"],["l",60,76,32,76,"a"],["l",60,76,88,76,"a"],["t",60,72,"x","m"],["l",180,76,92,76,"a"],["l",180,76,268,76,"a"],["l",60,76,32,76,"a"],["l",60,76,88,76,"a"],["t",60,72,"x","m"],["l",180,76,92,76,"a"],["l",180,76,268,76,"a"],["t",180,72,"(R − x)","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2023-q045","pn":45,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Gravitation","st":"Time period of near-earth satellite","q":"A satellite is orbiting just above the surface of the earth with period T. If d is the density of the earth and G is the universal constant of gravitation, the quantity 3π/(Gd) represents :","o":["√T","T","T²","T³"],"c":2,"s":"For a satellite just above the surface, T = 2π√(R³/GM).\nM = (4/3)πR³d, so T = 2π√(3/(4πGd)).\nSquaring, T² = 4π² × 3/(4πGd) = 3π/(Gd).\nSo the quantity is T²."}
